****
With their large widths, spectral lines Calcium K (2 nm) and Calcium H (1.5 nm) 
are ideal targets for any instrument, be that an etalon or a spectroheliograph. 

The notation is somewhat varied, Ca II (H), CaH, Ca II 3969, Calcium H -- we all know which Fraunhofer line we are talking about here.
Science is usually done on the K line instead, because it is somewhat stronger, and is further away from the hydrogen epsilon line, thus no fear of contamination.

Brighter prominences are visible through etalons of even 8nm, obvious through 3nm, but the disk is just plages and spots at these FWHMs.
The spiculae of the chromosphere are revealed by etalons downwards of 5&Aring;, but the dynamic range is still hard to manage, there is still too much
photosphere shining through.

The strenght of the spectroheliograph is that it can zoom into the K3 (or H3) line core, instead of showing 
the darkest center of the line blended together with the local valleys (1) and bumps (2). That is where filaments become obvious, 
spots get overwhelmed by the shining plages and active regions, where the Sun becomes eery in comparison to continuum.

The image above captures the light coming from the H3 line core.
